The calls and returns for `digit_sum(4096)`:

| call | waits for | returns |
|---|---|---|
| digit_sum(4096) | digit_sum(409) | 6 + 13 = **19** |
| digit_sum(409) | digit_sum(40) | 9 + 4 = 13 |
| digit_sum(40) | digit_sum(4) | 0 + 4 = 4 |
| digit_sum(4) | — (base case) | 4 |

Fast power in C++ — note the products stay below 10¹⁸ because both factors are reduced mod m (at most 10⁹):

```cpp
long long power(long long a, long long b, long long m) {
    if (b == 0) return 1 % m;
    long long half = power(a, b / 2, m);
    long long result = half * half % m;
    if (b % 2 == 1) result = result * (a % m) % m;
    return result;
}
```

Decode Ways: `decode(i)` = number of ways to decode `s[i:]`. Either take one digit (if it isn't 0), or two (if they form 10–26):

```cpp
long long decode(int i) {
    if (i == (int)s.size()) return 1;      // decoded everything: one way
    if (s[i] == '0') return 0;             // no letter starts with 0
    if (memo[i] != -1) return memo[i];
    long long total = decode(i + 1);
    if (i + 1 < (int)s.size() && (s[i] - '0') * 10 + (s[i + 1] - '0') <= 26) total += decode(i + 2);
    return memo[i] = total % MOD;
}
```

The same table filled from the back in Python, with no recursion:

```python
ways = [0] * (n + 1)
ways[n] = 1
for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):
    if s[i] != "0":
        ways[i] = ways[i + 1]
        if i + 1 < n and int(s[i:i + 2]) <= 26:
            ways[i] += ways[i + 2]
        ways[i] %= MOD
```

For `"226"`: ways[3] = 1, ways[2] = 1 ("6"), ways[1] = 2 ("2 6", "26"), ways[0] = ways[1] + ways[2] = 3.
